# What boot starts on the Raspberry Pi 5 once the first stage, the
# inittab's, has mounted the disks: from the system volume once it is /,
# or else from the initramfs. A shell on the debug UART, the first,
# which boot waits for, so that its exit stops the system.
#
# Then the drivers and services of the second stage, which the
# inittab's dev lines let drive their devices from either. rtc clock
# serves clock, the time, from the RTC in the Pi's power controller,
# which it asks the firmware for through the mailbox; it keeps time over
# a power cut only with a battery on the RTC connector. audio, the
# audio server, owns the sound devices its drivers attach, mixes every
# stream into each output and serves audio, which play, speak, rec and
# speech use; it plays the chime, /etc/chime.wav, once on the first
# output. usb-audio, of the USB devices xhci and the inittab's usb-hid
# share, reads every USB audio device's streams, a microphone's and a
# speaker's, and attaches them to audio. hvs drives HDMI0 through the
# HVS and serves display; it reads the HVS only if the firmware brought
# a monitor up on HDMI0 at boot, since otherwise the HVS is unclocked;
# to look at the HVS a stage at a time, start /sbin/hvs probe, list or
# iommu, each of which exits once done. hdmi-audio waits for the
# display, then attaches HDMI0 to audio as hdmi0 and plays what audio
# feeds it through dma40's channel 6: play /etc/chime.wav. gem drives
# the Ethernet port, RP1's GEM and the
# Broadcom PHY behind it, with the board's address from the firmware,
# and serves nic. rng200 drives the BCM2712's random number generator
# and serves random, as virtio-rng does on QEMU, for TLS's keys and the
# secret TCP's first sequence numbers are keyed with; it starts before
# inet, which asks random for that secret at its first connection.
# inet listens to gem and serves inet at the address a DHCP server
# gives it, as on QEMU.
# ntp waits for inet, which registers once DHCP has given it an address,
# asks the NTP server DHCP named, or pool.ntp.org, the time, and has rtc
# clock take it, writing it to the RTC; again every hour. A board with
# no battery on the RTC connector is in 1970 until it has.
#
# Then the desktop: font, the font service, which gives every program
# with a window its text's glyphs and starts before wm, which asks it
# first; and wm, which waits for the display hvs serves. No app is
# started here, so the desktop starts empty, and an app is started from
# the launcher. agentd, the agent's server, last: with no
# /etc/agent/config it says so and exits (make agent-etc makes one),
# and otherwise waits for its home's volume, the card's data
# partition, /Volumes/data, and serves agent, which run agent talks to.
# speech after it: with no /Volumes/data/speech/whistle.cact, which the
# installer fetches, it says so and exits; otherwise it reads Whistle,
# 14.6 s, and says what the microphone hears while F12 is held to the
# agent.
start /bin/sh
start /sbin/rtc clock
start /sbin/audio
start /sbin/usb-audio
start /sbin/hvs
start /sbin/hdmi-audio
start /sbin/gem
start /sbin/rng200
start /sbin/inet dhcp
start /sbin/ntp
start /sbin/font
start /sbin/wm
start /sbin/agentd
start /sbin/speech
